proposedIssues.body:
## Missing step-9 behavior

The inference cut-over's client step 9 requires the automation-rule editor and plugin grant surface to write actor-specific connection bindings. The server/router already support `automation-rule` and `plugin-grant` actors, and domain tests cover their ownership/refusal rules, but the production client constructs neither actor shape. Rules therefore fall back to the author's user bindings, while plugins have no grant-time route to the per-task rows the design requires.

## Done when

- the rule editor lists the rule author's compatible connections per routable task and reads/writes `connection.listBindings` / `setBinding` with `{ kind: "automation-rule", ruleId }`;
- the plugin grant surface lists only tasks the plugin declared and reads/writes the installing user's `{ kind: "plugin-grant", pluginId }` bindings;
- unavailable, background-refused, unset, deleted-connection, loading, error, and cross-owner refusal states are rendered and tested through real tRPC doors;
- plugin execution never silently inherits a user's defaults, while rule fallback follows the current actor-to-user fold;
- focused server and component tests pin the actor id and task carried by every write.

## Evidence

Current structural scan: zero actor objects for either kind across 625 TS and 694 TSX production client files, zero skipped. Server doors: `packages/server/src/transport/trpc/routers/connection.ts`; ownership/fold tests: `tests/server/domain/connection/verbs/bindings.int.test.ts`; active spec: `docs/design/orbweaver-inference-package.md` sections 5.3a, 7.1, and step 9.

## Missing step-9 behavior

The capability panel still resolves only the user's chat binding and cannot say which role/connection it describes. Provider degradation warnings are exhaustively translated, but every bus warning becomes a toast and runner warnings fan out independently each turn. Step 9 requires an explicit connection switcher, ambient capability-panel homes for sampling/effort/conflict warnings, and at most one turn notice when the dropped set changes for that connection.

## Done when

- the panel switcher defaults to the chat binding, can inspect another role/connection, and names the role, provider, and model in view;
- sampling-knob, effort, and conflict drops have an ambient home on that selected connection's capability panel;
- a turn emits at most one aggregated notice, and only when the dropped set differs from the preceding turn on that connection;
- `custom_parameters_ignored` remains visible at the offending Extras row before a turn, `declared_overrides_measured` remains a connection-row/editor badge, and background/arbitration degradation retains one session-level notice;
- switching connections, stale/deleted bindings, pending/error reads, and warning-set changes are covered in rendered tests through canonical reads.

## Evidence

`packages/client/src/features/preset/surfaces/preset-editor-surface.tsx` calls only `resolveChatCapability`; `packages/client/src/features/chat/components/chat-content.tsx` toasts each event; `packages/server/src/domain/chat/engine/engine.ts` fans independent runner warnings. Active spec: inference design section 5.3a and step 9.

## Missing step-9 behavior

The server persists and returns each assistant swipe's connection id, provider id, and model, but the room UI only offers an optional model glyph and uses connection/provider solely to qualify the OpenRouter cost readout. The composer has no quiet `running on …` line, and shared rooms do not explain that generated pictures live in the room or offer the specified room gallery filter.

## Done when

- the composer names the connection/model the next turn will use, with honest unset, blocked, and host-funded shared-room states;
- every assistant swipe can reveal the generating connection/provider/model from the persisted variant snapshot, and attribution survives connection rename/deletion without inventing current routing;
- shared rooms show `pictures you make here live in this room` at the image affordance and provide a room-scoped gallery filter;
- imported/user-authored/null-attribution rows, swipe changes, deleted connections, mobile density, keyboard/AT names, and mixed-provider rooms are covered through rendered tests.

## Evidence

`message-row.tsx`/`message-actions-row.tsx` render model only; `message-readout.ts` uses connection/provider only for cost eligibility; the current client corpus has no inference `running on` or shared-room image sentence. Active spec: inference design section 5.3a and step 9.

## Missing step-9 behavior and proof

The add dialog renders `claude setup-token` as prose rather than a copyable command, and the focused client corpus never drives a full add through credential mint plus connection creation. Step 9 explicitly requires CT per host and live drives for every built-in provider and three named personas. Existing #2549 owns diagnostics/verifyAuth and #2568 owns the hosted picker plus saved-key second-model action; this item owns the remaining provider-specific affordance and closed acceptance denominator without duplicating those implementations.

## Done when

- the Claude subscription arm provides a keyboard/AT-operable copy action for `claude setup-token` and keeps the machine-specific instruction;
- focused component coverage drives `credentials.add` then `connection.create`, including secret-free state and honest partial-failure recovery;
- the matrix covers every built-in provider and the OpenRouter one-pass/use-for-everything, subscription/background-refusal, endpoint/list-or-type, and built-in/keyless personas;
- evidence covers loading, empty, refusal, error, unavailable-provider, typed fallback, 870px, 486px, and mobile;
- the final matrix references #2549/#2568 receipts for their owned diagnostics and hosted-picker arms rather than reimplementing them.

## Evidence

`add-connection-dialog.tsx:205-219` has prose-only setup-token copy. Search of `tests/client/features/credentials` finds no add-flow `connection.create` call and only the saved-key replacement use of `credentials.add`. Active spec: inference design section 5.3a and step 9 receipts.
